i='TCS-3393';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Definition 3; Section 1.2.2, Theorem 7, Proposition 8 and following open question; Definition 17.'
refs.append(dict(id='journal2023',title='Non-Disjoint Promise Problems from Meta-Computational View of Pseudorandom Generator Constructions',authors='Shuichi Hirahara',year=2023,url='https://theoryofcomputing.org/articles/v019a004/',pdf_url='https://theoryofcomputing.org/articles/v019a004/v019a004.pdf',locator='Definitions 1.3 and 2.1; Sections 1.2.1–1.2.2, especially Proposition 1.8 and the question on page 8.'))
issue='The source asks for a barrier but does not define a class of proof methods or reductions to obstruct, a permitted complexity assumption or conclusion, or a criterion that makes an obstruction count. It also leaves the approximation polynomial implicit in the shorthand family. Fix these choices and their quantifiers before treating the request as one yes/no benchmark. Do not replace the request by proving NP-hardness itself or by proving non-NP-hardness under a selected reduction.'
save(i,dict(title='A barrier to NP-hardness proofs for non-disjoint Kolmogorov promise problems',formal='Is there a mathematically specified barrier explaining the difficulty of proving NP-hardness of Gap(K^SAT versus K), the source’s potentially non-disjoint promise-problem family? For an approximation polynomial τ, its yes instances are (x,1ˢ,1ᵗ) with K^{t,SAT}(x)≤s, and its no instances are those with K^{τ(|x|,t)}(x)>s+log₂τ(|x|,t). The yes side allows a SAT oracle and the no side does not. A barrier must concern this pair of sets, including its possible overlap. The source does not prescribe an obstruction theorem or a class of methods to which it should apply, so the requested barrier is not yet a complete yes/no proposition.',definitions='Fix an efficient universal oracle Turing machine U. For a finite binary string x, a positive integer t and an oracle A⊆{0,1}*, define K^{t,A}(x)=min{|d|: U^A on binary program d outputs exactly x within t steps}, with minimum +∞ if there is no such program. K^t means the same machine without an oracle. SAT is the language of satisfiable Boolean formulas in a fixed standard binary encoding. The program length and runtime are different resources; t is supplied in unary, as is the nonnegative length threshold s. The input length is Θ(|x|+s+t) in a standard tuple encoding. The approximation parameter τ:N×N→N is a fixed positive integer-valued polynomial; τ(|x|,t) sets both the no-side time bound and the logarithmic gap. The source writes Gap(K^A versus K)∈P when some such polynomial τ admits a polynomial-time separator. A separator must accept every yes instance and reject every no instance; the requirements are contradictory on an overlap, which is not discarded as an ordinary off-promise input. Under deterministic polynomial-time many-one reductions, SAT reduces to the pair when a total map R sends satisfiable formulas into the yes set and unsatisfiable formulas into the no set. This mapping condition can hold even if the target has no separator. The source’s surrounding discussion also considers weaker and randomized hardness notions, so the reduction class and the quantification over τ must be part of a proposed precise barrier. EXP is deterministic time 2^{n^{O(1)}}; ZPP is zero-error randomized expected polynomial time.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='First specify the formal statement meant by a barrier, including its method or reduction class, assumptions and approximation-polynomial quantifiers. A complete answer then proves that precise obstruction or refutes the corresponding universal obstruction claim. The general request for some explanation has no fixed negation or acceptance criterion as currently phrased. A barrier for the ordinary disjoint GapMINKT problem does not automatically answer this question.',context_blocks=[dict(title='Non-disjoint is a deliberate feature',text='The paper studies pairs of yes and no sets that are conjectured to overlap. If they do, no separator can meet both requirements, but ordinary mapping conditions for a many-one reduction can still hold. Treating overlap as irrelevant off-promise behavior would change the problem.',citation='journal2023'),dict(title='The familiar non-hardness consequence behaves differently',text='For the disjoint GapMINKT problem, the source discusses the consequence that deterministic many-one NP-hardness would imply EXP≠ZPP. Proposition 1.8 shows that proving the analogous implication for Gap(K^SAT versus K) would itself prove EXP≠ZPP unconditionally. Thus the same statement cannot simply be imported as comparable evidence against establishing NP-hardness here.',citation='journal2023'),dict(title='Why this metacomplexity question matters',text='The source relates hardness of these oracle/non-oracle compression problems to worst-case versus average-case complexity and to pseudorandomness. Its 2023 version records further converse results concerning the family with oracles throughout the polynomial hierarchy, while retaining the barrier question. Those implications are progress on the surrounding theory, not a specified obstruction theorem answering this request.',citation='journal2023')],why='Could identify which obstacles to reductions survive when a metacomplexity target is allowed to have overlapping yes and no sets, clarifying the scope of connections between worst-case hardness, average-case hardness and pseudorandomness.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2020',text='Proposition 8 distinguishes this target from the usual non-NP-hardness consequence for GapMINKT; the source asks for another barrier.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2023',text='The journal version retains the question after Proposition 1.8 and adds discussion of subsequent converse results for the broader oracle family.',citation='journal2023')],status='source_open',status_note='Read the conference and journal definitions, hardness discussion and exact open-question paragraphs. Focused searches through 12 September 2026 found no later result specifying and settling this general barrier request. The missing predicate remains a formulation issue, separately from current scientific status.',model_self_contained=False,requires_context=True,statement_review=dict(reviewed_on=DATE,status='needs_specification',notes=['Recovered Gap(K^SAT versus K), both time bounds and the unusual overlap semantics.'],remaining_issue=issue),importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='Examines the applicability of hardness-proof barriers to metacomplexity formulations tied to worst-case/average-case equivalences. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),review_note=issue,working_summary=dict(sentences=['The target compares short SAT-oracle descriptions of a string with longer descriptions that have no oracle.','Its yes and no sets may overlap, so a conventional separator may not exist even though mapping reductions can be defined.','The source asks for a formal barrier to proving NP-hardness of this target.','A standard barrier statement for a disjoint minimization problem would imply an unconditional complexity separation if transplanted here.','The source still does not fix the obstruction, reduction class or approximation quantifiers needed for one complete benchmark.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','journal2023'])),notes=['Restored the named non-disjoint promise family and exact asymmetric Kolmogorov definitions.','Defined unary parameters, separator versus mapping reduction and overlap behavior.','Explained Proposition 8 without turning it into the requested barrier or a solution hint.','Checked the 2023 journal update; left the missing barrier predicate and scope explicit.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs],state='improved_unfinished')
